The SSR story's cue groups
==========================

What the storyboard (`storyboard.lam`) says again and again, said once: the package `godot_ssr_story`, which
the storyboard imports (`$$[Import* godot_ssr_story]`; tools/video/build.py gives it to the reader).

$[Import* std]$[Import* content]$[Import* present]$[Import* render_lab]$[Import* godot_ssr]

A beat's opening keys: its demonstration and panel, the callout and the insets cleared; `more` are the beat's own.

$[# {opening demo panel more} {append {' demo {callout ""} {inset ""} panel} more}]

The room as the story shows it: a polished metal floor, no sky in the reflections, SSR on, every stage drawn, and no height field or cross-section over it.

$[# baseRoom {append
    {append
        {'
            {floorMetallic 1.0}
            {skyReflection 0.0}
            {ssrEnabled 1.0}
            {ssrComposeStep 3.0}
            {floorRoughness 0.15}
            {pickX -1.0}
            {pickY -1.0}
            {pickWidth 0.0}
            {pickHeight 0.0}}
        {view "field" {'
            {fieldShow 0.0}
            {fieldMorph 0.0}
            {fieldLevel 0.0}
            {fieldZoom 0.0}
            {fieldTurn 0.0}
            {fieldTilt 50.0}}}}
    {view "profile" {' {profileShow 0.0}}}}]

A camera low over the floor, the teapot and the sphere reflected in it.

$[# lowCamera {'
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.55 -0.6}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 10.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.6}}]

The march's worked example: P on the inside of the teapot's spout, whose ray crosses the gap to the teapot's body (far
wall's low columns, then the body's tall ones). A camera close beside the teapot, the spout clear of the trace's inset.

$[# spoutCamera {'
    {cameraTarget {' -0.15 0.38 -1.7}}
    {cameraYaw 35.0}
    {cameraPitch 12.0}
    {cameraDistance 1.8}}]

P is an SSR pixel, and its walk is a property of the pixels: a pyramid of other cells walks otherwise. So the spout's
beats render the scene at a size of their own (`frameWidth`, `frameHeight`: SSR at half of it, 550 by 540), fitted
beside whichever panel and at any resolution; the trace demonstration has the same `frame` (demos/trace.toml), so the
viewer walks the same pixels. At that size, P (366, 294) walks 0m 1m 2m 3m 4h 3h 2m 3h 2h 1m 2h 1h 0h (level, miss or
hit: thirteen iterations, a hit on the body near the lid), and (398, 277), at the spout's tip, heads away from the
camera and climbs off the top of the screen in five misses, into the sky. The narration states those walks: a change to the scene, the camera, the size, or the
trace changes them, so check them again (the SSR selftest's walk check).

$[# depthFrame {' {frameWidth 1100.0} {frameHeight 1080.0}}]
$[# spoutPick {append depthFrame {'
    {pickX 366.0}
    {pickY 294.0}
    {pickWidth 550.0}
    {pickHeight 540.0}}}]
